      titleEn: 'Zhuangyuan Tower and Yuan Jiagu',
      text: '拓东路东段旧时建有聚奎楼，民间俗称魁星楼，供奉掌管文运的魁星。清光绪二十九年（公元1903年），石屏人袁嘉谷赴京赶考，一举考取经济特科第一，成为云南历史上唯一的状元。消息传来，昆明城万人空巷，当地人引以为豪，便将聚奎楼改称为状元楼。此楼为三层石拱阁楼，高悬“大魁天下”金字匾额，车马行人皆从拱洞穿行，是城东最醒目的地标。1953年，因拓宽马路，矗立三百余年的状元楼被拆除。2013年，状元楼异地复建，重现老昆明记忆中的文化地标。',
      textEn: 'In the eastern section of Tuodong Road once stood the Jukui Tower, popularly known as the Kuixing Tower, dedicated to Kuixing, the deity in charge of literary fortune. In 1903, the 29th year of the Guangxu reign of the Qing Dynasty, Yuan Jiagu from Shiping County went to the capital for the imperial examination and won first place in the Special Economic Examination, becoming the only zhuangyuan (top scholar) in Yunnan\'s history. When the news arrived, Kunming erupted in celebration. Proud of this achievement, locals renamed the Jukui Tower to Zhuangyuan Tower (Top Scholar Tower). This three-story stone arch tower, with its golden plaque reading "Great Champion Under Heaven," stood as the most iconic landmark in the eastern part of the city, with carriages and pedestrians passing through its archway. In 1953, after standing for over 300 years, the tower was demolished to widen the road. In 2013, Zhuangyuan Tower was rebuilt at a new location, reviving a cultural landmark deeply etched in old Kunming\'s memory.',
