      textEn: 'During the Dali Kingdom period, the Dizang Temple (Ksitigarbha Temple) was built in the Tuodong Road area. Yuan Douguang, the prime minister of the Dali Kingdom, commissioned the construction of the Dizang Temple scripture pillar, also known as the "ancient pillar," to perform memorial rites for the deceased Marquis Gao Mingsheng of Shanshan. Carved entirely from a single block of stone, the seven-story, eight-sided pillar features over 280 Buddhist statues — some as tall as a meter, others only a few inches — each exquisitely carved with distinct expressions, representing the pinnacle of Dali Kingdom stone sculpture art. As centuries passed, the temple was destroyed in warfare, and the pillar was buried and forgotten. It was not until the Republican era that the ancient pillar was rediscovered. In 1923, the temple ruins were converted into Guzhuang Park, a popular leisure spot for old Kunming residents. Today, this national treasure is housed in the Kunming Museum on Tuodong Road, serving as the museum\'s most prized artifact.',
